5. Highest-Leverage Unknowns

Ranked by likelihood of determining success or falsification:

Q_pump vs Q_leak under actual tubing/head conditions
Determines whether restoration can dominate leakage.
Rod/bushing friction under wet cyclic load
Determines whether vertical motion is freely buoyancy-driven or guide-limited.
Pump-forced-motion risk
Determines whether the drum is actually moving by buoyancy transition or by pump thrust/tubing/vibration.
Partial-fill behavior
Determines whether the drum commits to state transitions or stalls in ambiguous buoyancy states.
Trapped air migration
Determines whether buoyancy state remains repeatable.
Stroke timing variance
Determines whether motion is bounded or drifting.
Restoration lag
Determines whether recovery phase remains phase-aligned.
Slosh-induced tilt / asymmetric flooding
Determines whether internal fluid motion causes side-load and rod bind.
Rebound at stroke reversal
Determines whether endpoint motion corrupts state separation.
Six-inch masking effect
Determines whether short-stroke success hides instability that would appear at larger stroke.
6. Current Attractor Hypotheses
Classification	Evidence signature
Stable attractor candidate	Repeated cycles remain bounded in stroke, timing, restoration delay, friction, and fill state without manual intervention. Perturbations decay back toward the same envelope.
Metastable behavior	Several cycles appear bounded, but partial-fill states, trapped air, timing drift, or friction sensitivity emerge intermittently. System survives temporarily but depends on narrow conditions.
Transient coherence	Early cycles look rhythmic, then stroke, timing, restoration, or valve/fill state drifts. Motion appears organized but degrades.
Divergence toward disorder	Rod bind, leakage growth, pump mismatch, partial-fill lock, rebound corruption, or side-load accumulation increases until cycling fails.

Current evidence state does not select among these. Simulation may explore signatures, but physical measurement remains required.

